WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.331
Go modules make dependencies visible, but
visibility is not the same as governance.

2
00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:07.973
Every dependency should have a reason to
exist, an owner, a maintenance story,

3
00:00:08.053 --> 00:00:09.078
and a path for updates.

4
00:00:09.158 --> 00:00:11.586
A small library can still introduce
transitive code,

5
00:00:11.666 --> 00:00:15.228
behavior during build or generation,
license concerns, or operational risk.

6
00:00:15.308 --> 00:00:16.495
Version control matters.

7
00:00:16.575 --> 00:00:20.352
go.mod and go.sum should be reviewed with
the same seriousness as application code

8
00:00:20.432 --> 00:00:22.051
when dependency behavior changes.

9
00:00:22.131 --> 00:00:25.584
Teams should understand why a version is
pinned, when updates are expected,

10
00:00:25.664 --> 00:00:28.794
and how vulnerability notices or
maintenance concerns are handled.

11
00:00:28.874 --> 00:00:32.543
Minimizing unnecessary dependencies can
reduce the amount of code the team must

12
00:00:32.623 --> 00:00:32.947
trust.

13
00:00:33.027 --> 00:00:35.132
Builds are also part of the security
boundary.

14
00:00:35.212 --> 00:00:38.179
Build flags, generated code, embedded
files, container images,

15
00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:40.688
release artifacts, debug symbols, linker
settings,

16
00:00:40.768 --> 00:00:43.843
and environment-specific behavior can all
affect what is shipped.

17
00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:47.701
A release process should make those
choices visible rather than hiding them inside

18
00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:49.669
local commands or undocumented scripts.

19
00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:51.638
CI/CD checks can reinforce the baseline.

20
00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:55.063
Tests, race detection where appropriate,
static analysis, formatting,

21
00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:57.895
dependency review, vulnerability checks
where available,

22
00:00:57.975 --> 00:01:01.969
configuration validation, and artifact
signing or provenance controls can help catch

23
00:01:02.049 --> 00:01:03.613
risky changes before deployment.

24
00:01:03.693 --> 00:01:06.769
The checks should be meaningful,
repeatable, and owned by the team.

25
00:01:06.849 --> 00:01:08.846
Reproducible habits make response easier.

26
00:01:08.926 --> 00:01:11.624
When teams know exactly what source,
dependencies, flags,

27
00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:15.265
and artifacts went into a release,
investigation and rollback become faster.

28
00:01:15.345 --> 00:01:18.043
Supply chain hygiene is not a separate
paperwork exercise;

29
00:01:18.123 --> 00:01:19.958
it is part of dependable Go engineering.

30
00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:23.599
It also gives maintainers confidence that
a security fix was built, packaged,

31
00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:25.460
and deployed from the intended inputs.
